KVarN: never flush into a page another KV-cache group has taken (#208, the "!!!!" output).

KVarN flushes a finished request's last full block to int4 in the first metadata build after the
request leaves the batch, and evicts retired sinks to int4 whenever its pool runs dry. On this
model every KV-cache group shares one tensor per layer position, so by then the page may belong
to another group. Both runners write mamba state before they build attention metadata
(preprocess_state on V2, preprocess_mamba on V1), so when the page has become a request's mamba
state page, the late tile write lands on state that is already there. The state reads back as
NaN and the request prints token 0, "!", from then on. The trigger reported in #208 is two
requests prefilling in the same steps while one of them finishes, which is why holding the second
request until the first has emitted a token, PREFIX_CACHE=0, or CTX=long each make it go away.

The runner now hands KVarN the step's scheduled block ids (note_scheduled_blocks, called after
the block tables are updated and before the forward). Each KVarN builder collects the pages that
appear in any group other than its own, and its next flushing build releases whatever its pool
still holds for them without writing the tile: the finished request's pending block, or a retired
sink. Nothing is lost. Allocating a page evicts its prefix-cache hash, so no request can read that
tile again. The builder's own group is left out, because its tables carry prefix-cache hits on
its own blocks, and a hit on a finished request's last block needs that block's flush.

The first drop logs "KVarN: dropped the pending tiles of N block(s) on pages another KV-cache
group now holds (#208)" at INFO; the rest log at DEBUG.

This takes the mechanism from #208's kvarn-recycled-block-guard.patch (masterjuggler), keyed on
the scheduler's allocations instead of a per-step window over each request's mamba columns: the
window also covered the column before the running one, which align mode frees, so a legitimate
flush into that page after it was reused as an attention block would have been dropped.

Kind: own
What: both runners hand KVarN each step's block ids, so it drops (never flushes) what it still holds
  for a page another KV-cache group has taken: a late flush of a finished request's last block, or
  of an evicted retired sink, into another request's mamba state was the "!!!!" output (#208); the
  KVarN half is in `kvarn/files`
Upstream: none
Cut-against: 0.30.0
Retires-when: rides with KVarN

